{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'Known gaps|Residual|L[1-6]|round 14|review-brief/10|every command|R1|D1' .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\"","aggregated_output":"pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:79:\t// boundary that every command the reviewer starts ends before it does\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:81:\treviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands = \"review-brief/10\"\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:137:\treturn text + \" (j) the `evidence` of a `passed` criterion is of a class the criterion asks for and, when the criterion or tool belongs to a component, of that component (the Sealed evidence list names the module of each ref); (k) the `basis` of a mapping is a requirement or decision id of the scope's spec, such as R1 or D1.\"\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:343:\t\tline(\"- Let every command you start finish before you end, and do not leave anything running in the background: POSE ends what is left and records it, and a long run left behind costs time you could have used.\")\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:200:pose spec-transfer preview --source <xref> --destination <xref> --map R1=equivalent:R1 [--json]\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:350:- `spec-transfer` — transfere explicitamente a autoridade de uma spec entre projetos. `preview` emite um plano JSON determinístico, vinculado a digest, sem escrever arquivos. O mapa de requisitos é explícito (`R1=equivalent:R1`, `R2=withdrawn`, `-=pending:R3`); `apply` exige o mesmo digest e um `--authorize-project` para cada repositório afetado, e `resume` valida os planos e recibos salvos. A operação só é permitida quando origem, destino e cada projeto afetado adotaram review-policy schema 4 (`qualified_artifact_refs_version: 1`, `spec_authority_transfer_version: 1`). Dependências e memberships atuais passam a seguir o redirect verificado; a tool MCP `pose_spec_transfer_status` lê o journal somente dentro do projeto autorizado. `--mode reconcile-terminal` retira uma spec coordenadora para um executor que já está `done` com closeout terminal no próprio projeto: o arquivo do executor nunca é escrito (o apply só confere o digest), o mapa é N:M sobre todo requisito de origem e pode apontar outra spec com `destination_ref`, `withdrawn` exige `rationale` e `pending` exige `rationale` e uma spec alvo aberta, registrada no redirect schema 2 como `open_obligations`; `--map-file` lê o mapa em JSON. Motores sem esse modo recusam planos e redirects schema 2. Não há commits, pushes nem adoção automática de política. Specs divididas em seções sem um único `spec.md` são reportadas como não suportadas, sem reescrita.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1071:**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux o envio roda o adaptador sob um shim de run de review, o mesmo executável reentrado, que é o subreaper da subárvore do run: depois que o adaptador termina, num timeout e quando o envio termina, ele encerra e recolhe todo processo que restou nessa subárvore, mesmo um que abriu sessão própria e limpou o ambiente, e relata os pids encerrados por um pipe que o revisor não herda; o envio os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza. Quem embute o motor sem ativar o shim recorre a encerrar os processos cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); nas outras plataformas Unix só o grupo de processos é encerrado, e no Windows o adaptador roda num job object com encerramento ao fechar, que o envio termina depois do run, num timeout e, pelo limite do job, quando o envio termina. O brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, sob um lock que o sistema libera junto com quem o detém, e uma liberação conclui com qualquer run que a tentativa já registrou em vez de gravar outro. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\nPOSE.md:196:pose spec-transfer preview --source <xref> --destination <xref> --map R1=equivalent:R1 [--json]\nPOSE.md:346:- `spec-transfer` — explicit cross-project authority transfer. `preview` emits a deterministic digest-bound JSON plan without writing. Requirement mappings are explicit (`R1=equivalent:R1`, `R2=withdrawn`, `-=pending:R3`); apply requires the same digest plus one `--authorize-project` for every affected repository, and resume verifies the saved plan copies and receipts. Transfer is refused until both endpoints and every affected project adopt review-policy schema 4 (`qualified_artifact_refs_version: 1`, `spec_authority_transfer_version: 1`). Current dependency/membership links follow the verified redirect; the MCP tool `pose_spec_transfer_status` reads the journal only within its authorized project. `--mode reconcile-terminal` retires a coordinator spec onto an executor that is already `done` with a terminal closeout in its own project: the executor file is never written (apply only verifies its digest), the map is N:M over every source requirement and may target another spec with `destination_ref`, `withdrawn` needs a `rationale`, and `pending` needs a `rationale` plus an open target spec, recorded in the schema-2 redirect as `open_obligations`; `--map-file` reads the map as JSON. Engines without this mode refuse schema-2 plans and redirects. No automatic commits, pushes or policy adoption occur. Split-section specs without a single `spec.md` are reported as unsupported rather than rewritten.\nPOSE.md:954:**Optional worktree runtime.** `pose runtime start` starts, detached, a runtime for this worktree; `attach` and `status` handshake with it, `stop` drains it (`--drain <d>`, default 5s) and says how many requests in flight finished and how many were abandoned, `restart` is stop then start, and `serve` runs it in the foreground (`--idle <d>`, default 30m, stops an idle runtime; 0 never does). It is opt-in: no other command starts it, and every command runs standalone whether it runs or not. The runtime is identified by the user, the canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) and the Git common directory, so two worktrees of one repository, or two users, never share one; the handshake compares the engine, build, protocol and schema as well and an attach from another engine is refused naming the field. Its endpoint is a Unix domain socket (Linux, macOS) of mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, else `pose-runtime-<uid>` under the temporary directory, or `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), whose peer the operating system identifies before a byte is read; a link, a loose mode, another owner or a file that is not a socket is refused, and a socket nobody answers on is replaced. There is no TCP and no token. Slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers handshake, status and stop, confers no authority, signature or presence, and every restart has a new epoch, inheriting nothing. On Windows, which has no named-pipe implementation here, `status` reports `unsupported` and every command runs standalone. `status` exits 0 for running, standalone and unsupported and 1 for an incompatible runtime; `attach` also exits 1 when there is nothing to attach to. `--json` prints the document `pose.runtime.status/1`.\nPOSE.md:1074:**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\nPOSE.md:1078:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object that the dispatch terminates after the run, on a timeout and, through the job's limit, when the dispatcher ends. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:35:- R1: When the adapter exits, the dispatch shall end every remaining process of the run and wait for them within a bound before the cleanup inventory: on Linux through the review run shim, the subreaper of the run's subtree, and on Windows through a kill-on-close job object. Other Unix platforms, which keep the process-group kill, are carried by `pose-review-run-reaping-beyond-linux`.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:37:- R3: The review brief shall ask the reviewer to let every command it starts finish before ending.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:100:`pose delegate cancel` releases a stalled attempt that has no run, recording a failed run and its receipt. Brief template `review-brief/10`. The run record's `safety.limitations` names the processes the dispatch ended after the adapter.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:108:On Linux the pose command runs the adapter under the review run shim, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree and ends what the run leaves in it after the adapter exits, on a timeout and when the dispatcher ends; the subtree is the run's by construction. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to the search by the sealed-checkout environment entry, which a reviewer process can clear or set (L6). Outside Linux only the process-group kill applies (L2).\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:123:### Decision D1\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:124:- Basis: R1, R2\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:142:- 2026-11-24: implemented R1–R6. Each has a test that fails with its fix mutated out: the dispatch ends a process the adapter left in a session of its own (the test's leftover keeps writing into the runtime directory), the release records the failed run and receipt and removes the marker and its stall claim, a live owner is never released, and `review-brief/10` asks commands to finish while `/9` stays unchanged.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:144:- 2026-11-24: review round 1 (rrn-c5c082703d83c834) requested changes. N1: the reservation was published before the active marker, so a dispatch ended in between left an attempt nothing could release; the dispatch now marks the attempt active first and publishes the reservation after, and a stalled marker without a reservation is removed by the release. When marking itself fails, the dispatch still publishes the reservation and records the failed run and its receipt, keeping the contract of `pose-delegation-result-before-marker`. N2: `delegate ls` now names the abandonment cause and the release command, in text and JSON. N3: each process is pinned by a pidfd and its environment read again through it before SIGKILL, so a reused pid is never signalled. N4: a scan that cannot list `/proc` or read a process state fails; a process of this user whose environment cannot be read is counted and disclosed in the run record rather than skipped (L3). N5: the manual says the reaping is Linux-only. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out (N4's disclosure is not reproducible in a test: it needs a non-inspectable process).\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:152:- 2026-11-24: review round 8 (rrn-f19179878d867e9a) requested changes. N1: the environment entry is the reviewer's to set, so with the shim the dispatcher no longer searches by it; the shim's subtree is the run's by construction, and the search remains only for an embedder without the shim (L6). N2: the shim's report goes through a pipe passed as file descriptor 3, which the shim closes on exec, never through a path the reviewer can write. N3: a timeout sends the shim SIGTERM, and the shim ends the run's subtree before it exits; the group kill after the run stays the backstop. N4: the shim receives SIGTERM when its dispatcher ends (`PR_SET_PDEATHSIG`, checked against the dispatcher's pid at start) and ends the run, so a dead dispatcher never leaves a reviewer running. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:154:- 2026-11-24: review round 10 (rrn-ecfc61a1c2b009f6) requested changes. N1: the shim no longer counts a process of another user whose stat it cannot read (procfs mounted hidepid) as a possible run child; only an unreadable process of this user is. N2 (follow-up): the orphan scan lists each directory with a bound and reports a cut listing as a loss. N3 (follow-up): the shim reports a pid only once its signal was delivered, and its error is recorded in the run's limitations. N4 (follow-up): Technical risks, D1 and the fallback helper's comment describe the shim as the Linux path. N2 has a test; N1 and N3 need a hidepid procfs or an unkillable child.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:155:- 2026-11-25: round 11 first hit the Codex usage limit; its resume pointed the reviewer at the removed checkout of the first attempt, so a new run reviewed the same bundle (rrn-2b8c0f5ecc1b6196) and requested changes. N1: a shim that cannot be set up now fails the run instead of falling back to the environment search. N2 (follow-up): a child that already ended is not reported as ended by the shim. N3 (follow-up): `cancel` says when it completed an attempt with a run already recorded, with that run's status. N2 has a test that fails with it mutated out; N1 needs `os.Executable` or `pipe` to fail, and N3 is a message of the CLI branch stated untested in Known gaps.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:156:- 2026-11-25: review round 12 (rrn-1b7f5566a0a0e47e) requested changes. N1: the manual and the run record no longer claim a process-group kill on Windows, where the dispatch never ended the adapter's descendants; the limit is L2, and a containment profile that requires `process-group-termination` was and stays refused there. N2 (follow-up): the shim reports a pid ended only after it was signalled and reaped. N3 (follow-up): the recorded count is the shim's total, not the listed pids plus a placeholder.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:157:- 2026-11-25: review round 13 (rrn-0d9d3f3755d48f99) kept Windows as a blocker: R1 has no OS exception. By the maintainer's decision the Windows dispatch now ends the reviewer's tree: the adapter starts suspended, joins a job object limited to kill on close, and only then resumes, so no descendant starts outside it; the dispatch terminates the job after the run and on a timeout, and the kill-on-close limit ends the tree if the dispatcher dies. N2 (follow-up) stays declared as L6, the fallback of an embedder without the shim. N3 (follow-up): the shim reads /proc in batches under its deadline. The Windows test compiles for Windows but cannot run on this Linux host.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:158:- 2026-11-25: review round 14 (rrn-88cb9ca67527d00e) requested changes with N1–N5. By the maintainer's decision the spec closes on its core: R1 and R2 now state Linux (shim) and Windows (job object), and N2 (group emptiness on other Unix), N3 (naming what Windows and Unix group kills ended) and N1 (the embedder fallback, L6) moved to `pose-review-run-reaping-beyond-linux`. Fixed here: N4, the bound is checked per process in the shim's scan and in the fallback scan; N5, the abandonment run a release records is deterministic (no clock reading), so a release interrupted between the run's witness and its record records the same run again, which completes it, and the release tolerates only the loss of its own run's record. N5 has a test (`TestAReleaseInterruptedAfterTheWitnessCompletes`) that fails with the run made non-deterministic; N4 needs a process table slower than the bound.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:166:- R1 [satisfied] test:TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning, test:TestATimeoutEndsTheRunsOrphans, test:TestTheShimEndsTheRunWhenItsDispatcherEnds, test:TestTheAdapterDoesNotInheritTheShimReport, test:TestTheShimReportIsBounded, test:TestAChildThatAlreadyEndedIsNotReportedEnded, test:TestAnOrphanThatClearedItsEnvironmentIsEnded, test:TestAPidThatLostTheRunsEntryIsNotKilled, test:TestANonInspectableProcessStartedDuringTheRunFailsTheScan\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:174:### Known gaps\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:181:The dispatch ends the processes a run left running before its cleanup and names them in the run record; `pose delegate cancel` releases an attempt abandoned before its run was recorded; `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:192:### Residual risks\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:193:L1: on Linux a run's process escapes only while it keeps a live parent outside the run and neither the run's group nor its environment entry, for example one handed to a service manager; the cleanup inventory still fails a run whose files it keeps changing. L2: on other Unix platforms only the process-group kill applies. On Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object; its test (`TestTheReviewersJobEndsItsDescendants`) is built for Windows and was compiled and vetted here, but not run, since this host is Linux. L6: an embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to the search by the sealed-checkout entry, which a reviewer process can set. L4: a reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner, so its release rests on the operator's confirmation, not on proof. L3: a process of this user that made itself non-inspectable cannot be matched: one that predates the run is skipped and counted in the run record, and one started during the run fails the cleanup, so such a run is not concludable.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:197:Carried by `pose-review-run-reaping-beyond-linux`: group emptiness on other Unix, naming what Windows and Unix group kills ended, and the embedder fallback (L6).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
